"""Adds to the note beyond up-sets a dated section on the Lean proof of the lemma on lifts and on the prime 2, as it stands after
the referee's reading of the first form of the section (apply_addendum.py), and one line to the list of files.
usage: make_beyond2.py read-text-beyond.txt | apply_addendum2.py ADDED-AT READ-FILE NOON-FILE > beyond-up-sets.md"""
import sys
s = sys.stdin.read(); when = sys.argv[1]; read = open(sys.argv[2]).read().strip(); noon = open(sys.argv[3]).read().strip()
a = "\n## Files\n"
assert s.count(a) == 1
sec = f"""
## 7. Added {when}: the lemma on lifts in Lean, the prime 2, and what they settle

*{read}*

The lemma on lifts of section 2 is now proved in Lean. I fixed the statements before any Lean proof of them; a fresh instance wrote the proofs; another audited the statements and found no difference from what is meant. The files are in the shared library (`../../../lean-tiling/Pnq/Lift.lean`, `Lift1.lean`, `LiftMin.lean`), added after Noon had read the statements; {noon} Copies, my checks and the audit are in `lean-lift/` here. The lemma, with a proof in a few lines by counting roots (it does not need the degree of a field, as the reader's proof above does, and it is the proof that the Lean files follow), is written out in the paper, `../../../paper/minsize.pdf`, section "Larger groups". Until the referee of this section asked, nobody had searched for the lemma in print. I have now made a short search myself, seven requests, for it and for the lemma on signs below, and found neither (`lean-lift/search-in-print.md`, with the nearest things found); that is weak evidence, and lemmas as simple as these may well be known.

**What Lean certifies** (`lean-lift/Lift.lean`, `lean-lift/LiftMin.lean`; r is a prime, and odd where it says so):

- `sum_reduce` and `cyclotomic_dvd_reduce_iff`: reducing a multiset on Z_{{N·r}} modulo N keeps its size and, for every d dividing N, whether Φ_d divides its mask (for every prime r, 2 included).
- `exists_lift`: for r odd, every nonempty multiset on Z_N is the reduction of a multiset on Z_{{N·r}} whose mask is divisible by no Φ_d with d dividing N·r and not N. This is the first lemma of section 2 in the form "some lift will do"; the particular lift is in the Lean proof (`Aux.exists_lift_aux`, `Aux.not_cyclotomic_dvd_of_single`), which are the proving instance's lemmas and were not among the statements fixed or audited.
- `sizes_eq`: for r odd and a set P of divisors of N, the sizes of the nonempty multisets with exactly the pattern P are the same on Z_N and on Z_{{N·r}}.
- `minsize_unbalanced_lift`, `minsize_balanced_all_lift`, `minsize_balanced_lift`: the three statements of the theorem for Z_{{pⁿq}}, on Z_{{pⁿq·r}} with r an odd prime, for the multisets whose pattern is made of divisors of pⁿq and has no other divisor of pⁿqr.

The reduction used for r₁ and r₂ in section 1 is thus in Lean for one prime step, 2 included (`sum_reduce`, `cyclotomic_dvd_reduce_iff`). Not in Lean: the prime 2 (the two lemmas below), the use of the lemmas for several primes in turn, the second lemma of section 2 (translates), the five moves beyond lifting, LB, nor anything else that is new in this note (what Lean has of the earlier theorems is said in section 1).

**The prime 2.** For r = 2 the particular lift of section 2 does fail (3 of 200 random multisets there), as it may: the proof uses 2·r^k ≤ (r − 1)·r^k. What is needed is less, that some lift has no new divisor, and for the groups of this note that is now proved, on paper. It comes in two steps.

*From lifts to signs (mine).* Write N = 2^k·N′ with N′ odd. An element of Z_N has two lifts to Z_{{2N}}, a low one and a high one (their residues modulo 2^(k+1) differ by 2^k). Give every element a sign, + for low and − for high. For d′ dividing N′, the polynomial Φ_{{d′·2^(k+1)}} divides the mask of the lift exactly when, for every class modulo 2^k, the signed mask of the elements in that class, taken modulo N′, is divisible by Φ_{{d′}}; this is the count of roots again, with 2^k roots and a polynomial of degree below 2^k. So it is enough to sign the elements of one nonempty class so that their signed mask on Z_{{N′}} is divisible by no Φ_{{d′}}, d′ dividing N′ (d′ = 1 included: the signs must not add up to 0), and to lift the other classes anyhow. For N odd there is one class, and lifting along 2 and signing are the same question.

*The lemma on signs (not mine).* I put that question, with what I knew, to a fresh instance, to prove or refute (`lean-lift/brief-sign-lemma.md`). It proved it for n = p^a·q^b, with p and q odd primes and a, b ≥ 0, and for n = p·r·q^b with three different odd primes; together these cover every odd n below 1155 = 3·5·7·11. Its text is `lean-lift/sign-lemma-result.md`, its programs `lean-lift/sign/`. In the first case the signs are chosen class by class modulo the powers of p, along the path from one element's class up to the whole group, so that modulo q the sum of all signs is not 0 and at each level two classes with the same parent have different sums; that this is enough follows from Φ_{{p^i·q^j}} ≡ Φ_{{p^i}} to a power, modulo q. The second case uses a signing of a rectangle of cells in which every row sum and column sum is 0 or ±1. A further instance, asked by that one, read its proofs and reported no error; I have read both proofs and found none, and the first case is written out, in a form of mine that needs one lemma less, in the paper's section "Larger groups" (Lemmas 17 and 18 there). For general odd n the lemma on signs is open, the instance says exactly where (its §6); the stronger form I had asked about, that all signs + or one sign turned is always enough, is neither proved nor refuted.

*Tested.* The lift along 2 built in this way, in an implementation of my own from the description (`lean-lift/two/lift_two.py`): on 1,400 multisets on 28 groups Z_N, N up to 150 with odd part of the first form, the lift to Z_{{2N}} had exactly the pattern of the multiset in all 1,400; the plain lift, every element low, had it in 1,117. Where nothing is proved, no counterexample has turned up either: the audit found a lift with no new divisor for every multiset it tried with r = 2 (by going through 2,530,899 multisets on the larger group in fifteen cases, N up to 30, and for 1,618,904 multisets on Z_N in eighteen cases, N up to 105); in my own searches on signs, all signs + or one sign turned was enough for every subset of Z_9, Z_15 and Z_21 that contains 0 and for 279,961 random subsets of Z_n for eight values of n up to 315 (the case of multisets reduces to sets; `lean-lift/sign-mine/`); and the instance found the same in 5,998, 5,998 and 2,999 random samples in Z_1155, Z_1575 and Z_3465, which its proofs do not reach.

**What that settles here.** The theorem for Z_{{pⁿq}} holds as it stands on Z_{{pⁿq·s}} for every s ≥ 1, for the patterns made of divisors of pⁿq: lift along the factors 2 of s first (the odd part of the group's order is then that of pⁿq, which has the first form), then along the odd primes, for which the lemma holds on every group. On Z_{{p²q²}} this gives the patterns among the divisors of p²q (with r = q the group Z_{{p²q·q}} is Z_{{p²q²}}) and those among the divisors of pq², for all p and q. So the 55 patterns of "In short", for which that theorem gave only a lower bound, are now theorems on all three groups. On Z_225 all 55 rest on the lemma that is in Lean. On Z_36 and on Z_100 that is so for the 31 that lie inside 12 (inside 20), where the lift is along 3 (along 5); the other 24, inside 18 (inside 50) and not inside 12 (20), need the lift along 2, which is proved on paper only. (When r = p the lifted statement is the old theorem one level up, as Noon pointed out; the new ground is r = q and a third prime.)
"""
s = s.replace(a, sec + a)
b = "`either.py` (the first tally, with three moves) ·"
assert s.count(b) == 1
s = s.replace(b, "`either.py` (the first tally, with three moves) · section 7: `lean-lift/` (the three Lean files, the statements as fixed, my checks, the audit, the brief; `brief-sign-lemma.md`, `sign-lemma-result.md` and `sign/`, the lemma on signs by the instance that proved it; `sign-mine/` and `two/`, my searches and my test of the lift along 2; `search-in-print.md`; `referee-lift/`, the referee's report on this section and its programs) ·")
sys.stdout.write(s)
